# ranger

A local LFM3-1.7B model analyses the codebase on your behalf. It starts from scout's output — candidate locations and the code graph, from Trailblazer when it watches the repo or from ranger's own index otherwise — reranks and verifies them with Grep/Glob/Read, and reports back its findings: file paths, line numbers, and a short summary. **Run scout first; use ranger when the locations alone do not answer the question.**

## When to use

- **Understand code** before editing, reviewing, debugging, or explaining it
- **Trace logic** across functions, files, or layers
- **Code Q&A** — "How does X work?", "Where is Y defined?", "What calls Z?"
- **Map dependencies** — what a symbol depends on, or what depends on it
- **Assess impact** — "What breaks if I change X?"

## When NOT to use

- You already read the exact file this session
- Single obvious grep in one known file
- Pure write/generate task with zero exploration needed
- The answer is already in Trailblazer (`trailblazer where`, `uses`, `impact`, `search`): those are faster and free
- scout's locations already answer it

## Usage

```bash
# Standard — send ranger to patrol a repo
python3 @SKILL_DIR@/ranger.py -C <repo> -q "<question>"

# Verbose — see each tool call and timing on stderr
python3 @SKILL_DIR@/ranger.py -C <repo> -q "<question>" --verbose

# Limit turns (default is 100)
python3 @SKILL_DIR@/ranger.py -C <repo> -q "<question>" --max-turns 20
```

## What to expect

- **Retrieval is scout's.** The candidates and graph are exactly what `scout.py` prints (see the scout skill): Trailblazer's graph and vectors when it watches the repo — started, and asked to watch, when needed — otherwise ranger's own index. With `--verbose` the line `candidates from trailblazer` or `candidates from index` says which.
- **Graph tools.** Where Trailblazer watches the repo the model also gets `Where` (where a name is declared) and `Uses` (every definition that uses it), which are exact where Grep is not.
- **Time per question** (measured 2026-10-03, CPU only, 6 threads): roughly 2 to 7 minutes; 3 of 3 test questions answered correctly with file and line.

The model runs on Mirai's local CPU llama.cpp router (`http://127.0.0.1:8094`, user service `mirai-llama-router`), which holds one copy of each model for her agent and for this skill — no API costs, no network calls, no GPU. Output is whatever the model says when it finishes exploring (typically a summary with file paths and line numbers). If it cannot connect: `systemctl --user status mirai-llama-router ranger-embed` and `docker ps` (Qdrant runs as the `ranger-qdrant` container).

## Do not repair this skill

Working as of 2026-10-22. It does not use ollama (there is none). If a run fails, do not edit `ranger.py`, `index.py`, `trailblazer.py`, the model names or the services; start what is down and retry:

```bash
systemctl --user start mirai-llama-router ranger-embed   # model router on 8094, embeddings on 8091
docker start ranger-qdrant                                        # index store on 6333
systemctl --user start trailblazer                                  # the code graph (ranger starts it itself when it is stopped)
```

Models are in `~/models`; the server binary is `~/vendor/llama.cpp/build-fresh`. ranger's source is `~/projects/ranger` — changes go there and are installed from there (`install.sh` installs ranger and scout into `~/.claude/skills` and `~/.agents/skills`), never by hand. If it is still broken after that, report it to the project lead instead of changing anything.